Git hooks:

Husky


Commit linting:

Commitlint



---

Repository Standards

Each package should contain:

src/
tests/
README.md
package.json
tsconfig.json

Avoid mixing documentation and implementation files in the same directory.


---

Folder Naming

Use lowercase with hyphens where appropriate.

Examples:

plugin-loader
context-engine
report-generator
knowledge-graph

Avoid ambiguous names like:

helpers
utils2
misc
temp


---

File Naming

Prefer descriptive names.

Good:

plugin-manager.ts
knowledge-node.ts
analysis-context.ts

Avoid:

manager.ts
index2.ts
new.ts


---

TypeScript Standards

Always enable strict mode.

Prefer explicit types for public APIs.

Avoid any except when unavoidable.

Example:

export interface AnalysisResult {
  duration: number;
  nodes: KnowledgeNode[];
}


---

Function Design

Functions should:

Perform one task.

Have descriptive names.

Avoid hidden side effects.

Return predictable values.


Example:

Good:

generateKnowledgeGraph()

Avoid:

doStuff()


---

Error Handling

Never silently ignore errors.

Use typed error classes.

Example:

class PluginLoadError extends Error {}
class AnalysisError extends Error {}
class ConfigurationError extends Error {}

Errors should include actionable messages.


---

Logging

Supported log levels:

TRACE

DEBUG

INFO

WARN

ERROR


Avoid console.log() in production code. Use the centralized logger.


---

Git Workflow

Main branches:

main
develop

Feature branches:

feature/plugin-prisma
feature/knowledge-graph
feature/rest-api

Bug fixes:

fix/context-engine

Documentation:

docs/api-spec


---

Commit Convention

Use Conventional Commits.

Examples:

feat(core): add plugin scheduler

fix(cli): handle invalid config

docs(prd): update roadmap

test(graph): add relationship tests

refactor(core): simplify pipeline


---

Pull Requests

Each pull request should include:

Summary

Motivation

Screenshots (if UI changes)

Tests added or updated

Documentation updates

Linked issue (if applicable)


At least one review is recommended before merging.


---

Testing Strategy

Minimum expectations:

Unit tests for core logic.

Integration tests for plugin interactions.

End-to-end tests for CLI commands.


Coverage targets:

Core Engine: ≥90%

SDK: ≥90%

Plugins: ≥80%

CLI: ≥80%


Coverage is a guide, not the only measure of quality.


---

Documentation Standards

Every public function should include TSDoc.

Example:

/**
 * Builds the project knowledge graph.
 *
 * @param context Analysis context.
 * @returns Generated knowledge graph.
 */

Complex architectural decisions should be recorded in documentation.


---

Dependency Management

Rules:

Prefer small, well-maintained dependencies.

Avoid duplicate libraries.

Remove unused dependencies promptly.

Review dependency licenses before adoption.



---

Performance Standards

Startup time:

CLI under 200 ms.


Analysis:

Medium projects under 30 seconds.


Memory:

Avoid unnecessary copies of project data.

Stream large datasets when practical.



---

Security Standards

Never log secrets.

Mask environment variables.

Validate all external input.

Sanitize file paths.

Follow the principle of least privilege for plugins.


Future releases will include automated security scanning.


---

Code Review Checklist

Reviewers should verify:

Correctness

Readability

Performance

Test coverage

Documentation

Error handling

Backward compatibility

Security implications



---

Release Process

1. Merge into develop.


2. Run CI pipeline.


3. Create release candidate.


4. Validate manually.


5. Merge into main.


6. Tag release.


7. Publish packages.


8. Publish release notes.




---

Decision Records

Major architectural decisions should be documented as Architecture Decision Records (ADRs).

Suggested structure:

docs/adr/
├── ADR-001-plugin-system.md
├── ADR-002-knowledge-graph.md
├── ADR-003-cache-strategy.md

Each ADR should explain:

Context

Decision

Alternatives considered

Consequences


This provides historical context for future contributors.


---

Contributor Journey

A new contributor should be able to:

1. Clone the repository.


2. Install dependencies.


3. Run tests.


4. Build the project.


5. Make a change.


6. Submit a pull request.



The process should take less than one hour for someone familiar with TypeScript.


---

Definition of Done

A task is considered complete when:

Implementation is finished.

Tests pass.

Documentation is updated.

Linting passes.

Type checking passes.

Code review is complete.

CI pipeline succeeds.



---

Engineering Culture

Ohira values:

Clarity over cleverness.

Reliability over speed of implementation.

Maintainability over short-term convenience.

Community collaboration over closed development.

Measured evolution over constant reinvention.



---

Guiding Principle

> "Great software is built by disciplined engineering, not just brilliant ideas."



Every contribution should leave the project in a better state than it was found.


